## Film 2: Dhurandhar: The Revenge (Part 2)
### Identity and release facts
**Official identity.** *Dhurandhar: The Revenge* (widely marketed as *Dhurandhar 2*) is the direct sequel and concluding entry in the Dhurandhar duology, written and directed by Aditya Dhar and produced by Jyoti Deshpande, Aditya Dhar, and Lokesh Dhar under Jio Studios and B62 Studios. It continues the story of undercover RAW agent Hamza Ali Mazari / Jaskirat Singh Rangi as he pursues revenge for the 26/11 attacks and confronts larger geopolitical threats.[60][61][6][1]

**Release profile.** The film opened in cinemas worldwide on **19 March 2026**, with paid preview shows on 18 March across India, after a multi‑month marketing build‑up anchored by teaser and trailer drops in early February and March. It is released simultaneously in **five Indian languages—Hindi, Telugu, Tamil, Kannada, and Malayalam—** marking a deliberate shift to a pan‑India strategy compared with Part 1’s Hindi‑only rollout.[62][63][1][12][8][44][64]

**Cast and crew.** Returning cast members include Ranveer Singh, R. Madhavan, Arjun Rampal, Sanjay Dutt, Sara Arjun, and Rakesh Bedi, with additional roles expanding the Pakistani and Indian security apparatus in the narrative; key crew (cinematography, editing, music, VFX supervision) also largely return, strengthening continuity in style and tone.[61][1][65][60]
### Macro analysis – expenditure account (Part 2)
#### Box office and pre‑release economics

Because *Dhurandhar: The Revenge* released on 19 March 2026, this report only covers **opening‑phase and pre‑release data up to the end of 19 March**:
- **Record paid previews:** Multiple trade outlets report that paid previews on 18 March alone generated approximately **₹43–44 crore net** in India, setting an all‑time record for Indian cinema, and surpassing even the full opening‑day collection of Part 1.[12][15][66]
- **Opening‑day collections:** Live box‑office coverage on 19 March suggests that combined paid previews plus first official day put the film around **₹100–140 crore net** in India and **₹160–200 crore worldwide gross**, though figures are still stabilising and vary by source as real‑time tracking updates roll in.[10][17][18]
- **Advance bookings:** Pinkvilla and other trade trackers report worldwide pre‑sales for the opening weekend finishing in the **₹187–200 crore** range, with over ₹100 crore in India and about USD 9 million (~₹75 crore) overseas booked before release, signalling extraordinary upfront demand.[7][10]

On the **cost side**, reports are more divergent:
- Some trade pieces state that the combined duology budget is around **₹300–350 crore**, as much of the sequel was shot alongside Part 1, implying a relatively modest incremental cost for Part 2.[23]
- Others, including box‑office blogs, estimate that the **Dhurandhar series is made on ~₹450 crore** for both parts, with Part 1 at ₹250 crore and Part 2 at around **₹280 crore**, though these may count P&A differently.[36]
- Additional regional‑language coverage in Kannada and pan‑India outlets cites **₹275 crore** for *Dhurandhar 2* alone and offers a wider rumour band of **₹250–475 crore** when including combined duology costs and VFX escalations.[22][24][37]

Given this spread, this report adopts **₹275 crore** as a **central working estimate** for Part 2’s production‑plus‑P&A cost, with a wide **₹250–350 crore range** labelled explicitly in tables.

#### Non‑theatrical and pre‑sold value

Non‑theatrical economics appear even stronger for Part 2 than for Part 1:
- Indian Express and trade analysis discuss how **streaming rights and associated deals for the duology together are in the ₹300–350 crore range**, with *Dhurandhar* itself sold to Netflix and the sequel expected to land with Jio’s own platform at a reported **₹150 crore** for digital rights alone.[23]
- A Sacnilk‑reported piece citing Pinkvilla notes that **non‑theatrical rights for Part 1 were around ₹140 crore**, whereas **Part 2 has locked a package worth about ₹245 crore**, combining digital, satellite, and ancillary rights—nearly doubling the non‑theatrical valuation of the IP.[19]

This means that **a large fraction of Part 2’s cost base is effectively covered before release**, especially when coupled with high‑margin duology economics where many core production expenses were already incurred during Part 1’s making.[14][19][23]

